	(text_box "CLK1 IS THE NULL-CHANNEL SOURCE.\nSet it to exactly 10.000000 MHz and patch this SMA to channel 13 with a short coax.  Round 28: CLK1\nruns from PLLB in INTEGER mode (25 MHz x 32 = 800 MHz, MultiSynth 80) and the helper from PLLA.\nSame TCXO, so the beat is exactly 1234.4 Hz; two PLLs, so what channel 13 reports is the front end\nplus the PLLs' uncorrelated noise - an UPPER BOUND on the floor, not 'noiseless' as earlier rounds\nsaid.  (CLK1 off PLLA would need MultiSynth 80.0099, the fractional mode the helper was steered away\nfrom.)  A cable rather than a jumper keeps every channel tile identical and puts a real connector in\nthe loop, which is the honest thing to measure."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 400.05 45.72 0)
		(size 170.18 24.13)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 220 235 255 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "3d2817b4-39d2-2f87-2179-d5e301fcfb84")
	)
	(text_box "WHY THE 74LVC1G34 HERE AND THE 74LVC1GU04 NEXT DOOR.\nThey are opposites on purpose.  The squarer needs the UNBUFFERED GU04 because it\nis run inside a 1M feedback loop at its own threshold, and one gain stage in\nthat loop cannot oscillate while three can.\nEverything after it wants the exact opposite: no feedback, no linear region,\njust the fastest, stiffest edge available into fourteen flip-flop clock inputs.\nThat is the BUFFERED G34 - three cascaded stages, ~30 ohm output, and an input\nslew-rate limit in its Recommended Operating Conditions that the squarer's clean\nedge already satisfies.  Using a GU04 here would give soft edges and multiply\nevery flip-flop's threshold mismatch straight into skew."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 431.8 166.37 0)
		(size 139.7 29.21)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 220 235 255 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "3f74e411-2dfe-827b-9373-66c7e02a821f")
	)
	(text_box "DRIVING XA WITH A REFERENCE IS A DOCUMENTED MODE.  Si5351 datasheet 6.6,\n'Applying a Reference Clock at XTAL Input': 1 Vpp AC-coupled into XA, XB open -\nwhich is this circuit.  (Rounds 17-27 said it was undocumented; two of round\n28's reviewers repeated that.)  The Si5351A has no CLKIN pin - only the C does -\nso XA is the input.  XA/XB are absolute-maximum -0.5 to +1.3 V and the pin's own\nbias point is not published: 0.77 Vpp (3k3/1k from 3.3 V CMOS, then the 10 nF)\nis the largest swing that stays under 1.3 V even if the bias is as high as 0.9\nV, and it is within 30 percent of the datasheet's figure.  Order matters: the 1k\nmust sit on the divider, not on XA.\nProbe TP_XA, on the DRIVEN side of the coupling cap where 10 pF of probe does\nnothing; probing XA itself would pull the oscillator.  If the part will not\nstart at 0.77 Vpp, 1k -> 1k3 gives 0.93 Vpp.  If it will not run at all, fit\nY3001 and drop the TCXO: +/-10 ppm, which cannot avoid the 60 Hz comb."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 210.82 45.72 0)
		(size 121.92 36.83)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 220 220 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "d8d51b24-f9bf-79d5-5d27-e51deb88b54d")
	)
	(text_box "HELPER SKEW ENTERS AT FULL SCALE, 1:1.  A delay d in one channel's helper branch\nshifts that channel's beat by d x N, which referred back to the input is d.\nOnly DIFFERENTIAL drift survives: a constant is a per-channel calibration the\nnull test removes, and a shift common to all fourteen branches cancels exactly.\nSo the copper barely matters, and it is worth doing the arithmetic rather than\nreflexively length-matching.  FR4 carries about 6.9 ps/mm and its delay tempco\nis 50-100 ppm/degC, so a branch MISMATCH of 100 mm - the worst this board could\nproduce - drifts 0.7 ps over a 10 degC swing, against a 12.3 ps single-shot\nresolution.  Match the branches to 10 mm because it is free, not to 1 mm; the\nlength itself is a per-channel constant the null test removes.\nSILICON is the whole problem, which is why the four second-stage buffers have to\nbe physically ADJACENT so their tempcos track.  That is the layout constraint,\nand it is the only one on this sheet that is tight."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 299.72 166.37 0)
		(size 121.92 36.83)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 220 220 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "ef2453e6-e9d0-46a9-bdf7-ecaa61f7ee30")
	)
	(text_box "WHY A 1:4 TREE AND NOT ONE GATE.  One 74LVC1G34 driving fourteen flip-flop inputs plus their traces is about 100 pF, which gives 5-8 ns edges.  Per-flip-flop clock threshold mismatch converts to skew\nas dV/slew, so a slow edge multiplies a 5 mV threshold difference into 12 ps instead of 3 ps.  Two stages keep every branch at 1-2 ns.  33R at each driver is series termination: at a 1 ns edge the\nbranch is a transmission line once it is longer than about 45 mm."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 15.24 345.44 0)
		(size 340.36 10.16)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 220 235 255 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "fe15985e-4d0e-dc24-6377-0988b4e8c237")
	)
